  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+3}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x+3 & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}\u2061x & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the solutions to $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x=1$ correspond to the values of $x$ at which $\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ attains its minimum value.\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\operatorname{arccos}\u2061(-1)={180}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${180}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={180}^{\u2218}.$\n\nThis solution is a quadrantal angle. To find the remaining solutions, we use our knowledge of the cosine function. We know that the cosine function has its minimum values at\n\n$$\nx={180}^{\u2218}+{360}^{\u2218}\\cdot n.\n$$\n\nSince the domain ${0}^{\u2218}\\le x<{360}^{\u2218}$ is one period, and we have already found one minimum value in this period, we conclude that $x={180}^{\u2218}$ is the only solution.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
